{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"update-stage28.json\", \"summary\": \"The legacy-encoding lead is now answered conditionally rather than left open, by settling the reachability precondition from source (valid:true).\\n\\nMechanism (already sourced): with a leader whose protocol version is below 2, `appendConfigurationEntry` writes a deprecated remove-peer entry carrying `encodePeers` output (raft.go:1219-1233), `encodePeers` keeps only Voter suffrage with the explicit comment that other suffrages are unsupported (configuration.go:311-327), and the receiving side decodes deprecated entries with `decodePeers`, reconstructing voters only (configuration.go:330-352). `checkConfiguration` still requires at least one voter, so nothing on that path reports the loss.\\n\\nNew this turn — reachability: `NewRaft` rebuilds the configuration by replaying the log through `processConfigurationLogEntry` (api.go:593-607), decoding each entry by its own type rather than by the node's configured protocol version, so a member restarted at protocol 1 keeps a configuration containing non-voters that was originally written in the versioned format. At that version the available membership API is exactly the deprecated one — `AddPeer` refuses above protocol 2 (api.go:905-919) while the versioned AddVoter/AddNonvoter require protocol 2 or above — and the RPC header check accepts one version back (raft.go:45-70), so a protocol-1 node can still exchange RPCs with protocol-2 peers that understand the deprecated entry. Therefore the loss is reachable in a rollback-style deployment: a member restarted below protocol 2 that leads a membership change drops every non-voting member from the appended entry, and every peer decodes it that way.\\n\\nConclusion recorded as conditional, with the trigger stated as a deployment requirement rather than assumed: only clusters that used the versioned configuration format and then ran a member below protocol 2 are affected; quorum cannot be lost because at least one voter is still required, and a cluster that never used the versioned format cannot hold non-voter entries at all. Whether rollback below the version that wrote a configuration is a supported operation is left as a policy question — the captured code neither prevents nor warns about it. Chinese report fields summarise the conditional finding for offline review, and no execution was requested, with that limitation recorded explicitly.\"}"}}
